<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kittery, Maine</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kittery is a town in southern Maine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piscataqua-river">Piscataqua River</a> opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portsmouth-new-hampshire">Portsmouth, New Hampshire</a>. Settled in the 1620s and incorporated in 1647, it is the oldest incorporated town in Maine. It became a centre of shipbuilding, and the Portsmouth Naval Shipyard stands on islands within its bounds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kittery-maine.jpg" alt="Kittery, Maine" width="320" height="187" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Greencan7 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lady_Pepperrell_House_Kittery_Point_Maine.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kittery lies in York County, at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piscataqua-river">Piscataqua River</a>, opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portsmouth-new-hampshire">Portsmouth, New Hampshire</a>. It is the southernmost town in Maine and includes Badger's Island, the seaside district of Kittery Point, the village of Kittery and part of the Isles of Shoals. Of its area of 195 square kilometres, about three quarters is water. Its population was 10,070 at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and it is a tourist destination known for its outlet stores.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>English settlement around the harbour of the Piscataqua estuary began about 1623. By 1632 the community was protected by Fort William and Mary on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a> side of the river, and in 1689 defensive works that later became Fort McClary were added on the Maine side. Kittery was incorporated in 1647 and was named after the manor of Kittery Court at Kingswear in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>, the birthplace of the founder Alexander Shapleigh, who arrived in 1635 on the Benediction, a ship he co-owned with Captain Francis Champernowne. With the Pepperrell family they established fisheries at the Isles of Shoals, where fish were caught, salted and exported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Other settlers hunted, trapped and worked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>.</p><p>Kittery developed into a centre of trade and shipbuilding. The township originally reached inland up the Salmon Falls River and included the present towns of Eliot, South Berwick, Berwick and North Berwick. In 1652, after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinando-gorges">Sir Ferdinando Gorges</a>, Maine became part of the Massachusetts Bay Colony. In 1663 John Josselyn called Kittery the most populous town in the province. The Maine Historical Society calls Sir William Pepperrell, who lived at Kittery, the state's most prolific and infamous slave owner, and as many as 500 enslaved people were brought to Kittery Parish in the colonial era. In 1705, during Queen Anne's War, Wabanaki raiders killed six residents and took five prisoners.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The first vessels of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a> were built on Badger's Island during the Revolution, among them the USS Ranger of 1777, commanded by John Paul Jones. The Portsmouth Naval Shipyard, the nation's first federal navy yard, was established in 1800. In the Civil War it rebuilt the USS Constitution and built the USS Kearsarge. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-portsmouth">Treaty of Portsmouth</a>, which formally ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a>, was signed at the shipyard in 1905.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kittery, Maine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Fishing stations and small settlements grew along the coast at <b>Kittery</b>, York and Saco, and in 1639 a royal charter made Gorges proprietor of the Province of Maine.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-maine">Province of Maine</a></li></ul></section>
